sequenceDiagram
  %% Target design: ADR 0244 task, DECISIONS R1-R4, 5 (swamp role SSH identity), round 2 bugs (server admin password by its own vault key; the owner password is staged and always removed), flow-tighten. docs/reference/database-servers.md.
  %% Tightened: flow-tighten 2026-11-02, rules 14, 16, 22 (one docker exec -i with SQL on stdin, no staged password or cleanup, no script read, report or assert step).
  %% Verified: the parallel test database pool applies to MySQL and PostgreSQL in CreateServerDatabaseAction.
  %% status: draft
  %% command: database:create
  %% kind: database.create
  autonumber
  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray
  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue
  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green
  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber
  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools
  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API
  participant SW as Swamp<br/>serve on Node 209
  participant N as Node 12<br/>beast-mysql server
  CLI->>GW: database:create shop
  %%> Command:
  %%> - `orbit database:create shop --driver=sqlite --path=/srv/shop.sqlite`
  %%> - syntax: `<slug> [--server=SERVER] [--instance=ID] [--locale=C|C.UTF-8] [--test-processes=N] [--idempotency-key=KEY] [--json]`
  %%> - or, to register an existing database: `<slug> --driver=DRIVER [--host=HOST] [--port=PORT] [--database=NAME] [--path=PATH] [--username=USER] [--password=SECRET] [--node=NODE]`
  %%> - on a Valkey server: `<slug> --server=SERVER --pattern=PATTERN ... --allow=RULE ... [--database=INDEX]`
  %%> Input:
  %%> - the connection slug as the argument: lowercase kebab, at most 63 characters
  %%> - with `--server`: the Database server that gets the new database
  %%> - with `--instance`: the Instance that owns the database and gets it attached under prefix `DB`
  %%> Who can call it:
  %%> - the `orbit` CLI and the MCP tool send the same request
  Note over GW: Validate registration
  %%> Requirements:
  %%> - only the slug is a required signature argument; registration also needs a driver and that driver's fields
  %%> - slug alone is refused with `database.driver_invalid` by the CLI
  %%> - the default path records an existing database; it creates no database or owner on a Node
  opt --driver
    Note over GW: Select the driver
    %%> What it changes:
    %%> - Choose mysql, pgsql, sqlite or redis; required for registration.
  end
  opt --node
    Note over GW: Associate a Node
    %%> What it changes:
    %%> - Resolve the supplied Node for the connection record.
  end
  opt --host
    Note over GW: Set the host
    %%> What it changes:
    %%> - Required for mysql, pgsql and redis registration.
  end
  opt --port
    Note over GW: Set the port
    %%> What it changes:
    %%> - Replace the driver default TCP port.
  end
  opt --database
    Note over GW: Set the database
    %%> What it changes:
    %%> - Required name for SQL registration; optional index for redis.
  end
  opt --path
    Note over GW: Set the SQLite path
    %%> What it changes:
    %%> - Required for sqlite registration.
  end
  opt --username
    Note over GW: Set the username
    %%> What it changes:
    %%> - Required for mysql and pgsql registration.
  end
  opt --password
    Note over GW: Set the password
    %%> What it changes:
    %%> - Required for mysql and pgsql registration; encrypt it in the record.
  end
  Note over GW: Record the connection<br/>R1: no run · password encrypted
  %%> Result:
  %%> - connection `shop` is recorded, with its password encrypted
  %%> - no task and no Swamp run start (R1); nothing changes on any Node
  GW-->>CLI: 201 Created<br/>connection shop
  %%> What you get:
  %%> - the connection record at once; there is nothing to follow
  opt --server
    Note over GW: Validate, reserve the name<br/>connection reserved · task #5121
    %%> Requirements:
    %%> - the slug `shop` is free (`database.slug_conflict`)
    %%> - with `--server`: Database server `beast-mysql` is active, and your Node has an access grant to Node 12
    %%> - `--locale` only on PostgreSQL; `--test-processes` on MySQL and PostgreSQL; `--pattern` and `--allow` only on Valkey
    %%> - no database removal runs on `beast-mysql`; the Gateway holds one lock per Database server
    %%> Builds:
    %%> - without an Instance: database `shop` and its own owner `shop`
    %%> - a new password for the standalone owner
    opt --instance
      Note over GW: Use the Instance owner
      %%> What it changes:
      %%> - Requires a MySQL or PostgreSQL server and existing Instance 42.
      %%> - Use the Instance owner and password, reusing them when already recorded on this server.
      %%> - Add the test database and default eight-worker pool to the same create batch.
      %%> - After native success, project the attachment to Instance 42 under DB; run `env:sync` to install its keys (on a container Instance it writes only the connection's keys in place).
    end
    opt --test-processes
      Note over GW: Set the test pool size
      %%> What it changes:
      %%> - Requires an Instance; replace the default 8 with 1–32 workers.
    end
    opt --locale
      Note over GW: Set PostgreSQL locale
      %%> What it changes:
      %%> - Use C or C.UTF-8 and template0 instead of inheriting the image default.
    end
    opt --pattern
      Note over GW: Set Valkey key patterns
      %%> What it changes:
      %%> - Required for a Valkey server; restrict the ACL user to these key patterns.
    end
    opt --allow
      Note over GW: Set Valkey commands
      %%> What it changes:
      %%> - Required for a Valkey server; replace the allowed command list.
    end
    opt --database
      Note over GW: Set the Valkey index
      %%> What it changes:
      %%> - On a server, only Valkey accepts this option; check it against the live database count.
    end
    GW->>SW: Store the owner password<br/>database-create-intent-5121
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - puts two whole values into the Swamp vault on Node 209, after the task is committed (R3)
    %%> - `database-create-intent-5121`: the admin password line, then the create batch with the owner password inside
    %%> - `database-create-owner-5121`: the owner password line, then the owner check
    %%> Why:
    %%> - secrets go Gateway → vault → step; workflow inputs carry only vault key names
    GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>operation-database-create
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - starts one Swamp run on serve (Node 209)
    %%> Inputs:
    %%> - the driver, database names, owner name, test pool size and locale
    %%> - the vault key names `database-create-intent-5121` and `database-create-owner-5121`
    %%> - Node 12's address and SSH host keys; the SSH identity comes from the `swamp` role
    GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>task #5121
    %%> What you get:
    %%> - the task number, at once
    %%> Next:
    %%> - the CLI follows the task with `operation:show`
    SW->>N: Create owner and databases<br/>docker exec -i · SQL on stdin
    %%> How:
    %%> - Swamp reaches Node 12 over SSH from 209 with the official `@swamp/ssh` behavior and the `swamp` role's SSH identity; nothing is copied to the Node
    %%> - one `docker exec -i` into the server container per statement batch, with the statements on stdin; the owner password from `vault.get` sits inside the SQL, never in argv or a file
    %%> - admin login: inside the container, the first stdin line is the admin password, read into the client's password variable, never in argv or a file
    %%> What it does, in order:
    %%> - creates the owner login, or keeps it when it already exists with this password
    %%> - creates the primary database and grants its standalone owner access
    %%> - grants the owner only the databases in the admitted batch
    %%> Refuses:
    %%> - a name the server already has for something else (`database.name_conflict`); the batch refuses by itself before any change, so no read runs first, and prints the refusal for the Gateway
    %%> Per driver:
    %%> - MySQL: one grant per database; the Instance block adds the test prefix grant
    %%> - PostgreSQL: the chosen locale and `TEMPLATE template0`; revokes PUBLIC access, then grants the public schema to the owner
    %%> - Valkey: `CONFIG GET databases`, then `ACL SETUSER shop reset on #<hash> resetchannels ~<pattern> -@all +<rule> -@admin -@dangerous -function -script`, then `ACL SAVE`; the Gateway checks index 1 against the live count
    %%> - Valkey with `--allow=flushdb`: `+flushdb` follows the final deny; give the user its own `--database` index
    SW->>N: Check the owner login<br/>docker exec -i · as the owner
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - logs in as the owner, password as the first stdin line, and reads its own name; the Gateway checks the answer
    %%> Why:
    %%> - the connection only counts as active when the server accepts it; this is the one end check
    %%> Not included:
    %%> - no staged password file, so no cleanup step; no report or assert step
    alt every step passes
      N-->>SW: owner logs in
      %%> Returns:
      %%> - the check passed for every database
      SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>terminal event
      %%> How the Gateway learns it:
      %%> - its shared run reader sees the terminal event; Swamp never calls the Gateway
      Note over GW: Record create success<br/>connection shop · task succeeded
      %%> Result:
      %%> - connection `shop` stays linked to `beast-mysql`, with Node 12's address and port
      %%> - task #5121 succeeds
    else a step fails
      N--xSW: name taken or exit ≠ 0
      %%> When:
      %%> - a name is taken, SQL fails, SSH fails, or the owner cannot log in
      SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>error_code, failed step
      %%> The Gateway reads:
      %%> - which step failed and its exit code, mapped to `error_code` such as `database.name_conflict`
      Note over GW: Keep the connection<br/>task failed · rerun after a fix
      %%> Result:
      %%> - connection `shop` stays reserved with its encrypted owner password
      %%> - an invalid Valkey index removes an unattached connection and its recorded user; the ACL user can remain on the server
      %%> - task #5121 fails
      %%> On the Node:
      %%> - an owner or database created before the failure stays; there is no rollback
      %%> - that is safe: the name check accepts objects that already belong to this connection
      %%> Retry:
      %%> - run the same command again; it reuses the reservation and finishes the missing steps
      %%> - a changed request is a conflict (`database.slug_conflict`)
    end
    loop every 2 s until terminal
      CLI->>GW: operation:show #5121
      %%> What it does:
      %%> - asks for the task's status every 2 seconds until it has ended
      GW-->>CLI: status + result
      %%> Returns:
      %%> - the current status, and the result once the task has ended
    end
  end
